"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][24]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M24-L1 – Text Representation, Tokenisation and Linguistic Features<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish tokenisation,\nlexical and linguistic features, and recognise information lost through\na representation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Text is not yet a numerical vector<\/h2>\n<p>A search system for repair reports should recognise related topics in\n“Die Pumpen liefen nicht” (“The pumps were not running”) and “Pumpe\nläuft” (“Pump running”), but must not lose the distinction between a\nfailure and normal operation. Unicode normalisation (e.g. NFC)\nstandardises canonically equivalent character strings. Case distinctions\nmay be unnecessary for some search tasks but informative for names and\nabbreviations. Words are not always delimited unambiguously by spaces:\nhyphens, technical identifiers and languages written without spaces\nbetween words require suitable processing rules.<\/p>\n<p>Lemmatisation maps an inflected word form to its lexical base form.\nStemming generally uses coarser truncation rules. Part of speech (POS),\nmorphological features, syntactic dependencies and named entities\nprovide additional information. A bag-of-words vector, by contrast,\ncontains only counts, so negation and syntax may be lost. Character\nn-grams help with spelling variants. Subword approaches such as Byte\nPair Encoding (BPE) learn frequently adjacent units and can represent\nrare words using smaller components. With subword tokenisers offering\ncomplete byte-level coverage, every byte sequence is representable, but\nnot necessarily efficiently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Building a miniature vocabulary<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001717_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three stages shrink the token count from 15 to 10 to 7 through two BPE merges.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three stages shrink the token count from\n15 to 10 to 7 through two BPE merges.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Byte-pair merges for subword tokenisation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider the artificial word forms “rad”, “rad”, “rad”, “rat”, “rat”.\nInitially, these consist of characters. The pair r–a occurs five times,\na–d three times and a–t twice. The first BPE step merges r–a into “ra”,\nand the second merges ra–d into “rad”. The diagram shows both steps.\nReal training must account for word boundaries, repetitions, a stopping\ncriterion and deterministic tie-breaking. BPE does not guarantee\nlinguistically meaningful morphemes.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> collections <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Counter<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>words<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(w) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> w <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&#39;rad&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;rad&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;rad&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;rat&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;rat&#39;<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    counts<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>Counter(p <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> w <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> words <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> p <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(w,w[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    a,b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(counts,key<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>counts.get)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    out<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> w <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> words:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> i<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> i<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(w):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> w[i:i<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[a,b]: v.append(a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> i<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>: v.append(w[i])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> i<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        out.append(v)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    words<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>out<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> words<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>[[<span class=\"st\">&#39;rad&#39;<\/span>]]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>[[<span class=\"st\">&#39;ra&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;t&#39;<\/span>]]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(words)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Features for different tasks<\/h2>\n<p>An index can store the original text separately from its normalised\nanalytical form. For sentiment analysis or diagnosis of operating\nstates, removing “nicht” (“not”) is risky. A neural language model uses\na fixed token-ID table and trainable embeddings; the numeric token ID\nhas no intrinsic numerical meaning. The tokeniser must remain compatible\nwith the model. Changing the vocabulary after training is not a trivial\npreprocessing operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many tokens remain after the two steps? There are 15 initially,\n10 after the first merge and seven after the second. The German texts\n“Motor läuft nicht” and “Motor nicht läuft” produce the same\nterm-frequency vector. Position features or contextual representations\ncan distinguish them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07909\">Sennrich et al. (2016):\nNeural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nabstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.elastic.co/docs/solutions/search/full-text/how-full-text-works\">Elastic:\nHow full-text search works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~jurafsky/slp3/index.html\">Jurafsky and\nMartin: Speech and Language Processing, 3rd ed., online manuscript<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsearch snippet only<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M24-L2 – Inverted Indexes, TF-IDF and BM25<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct an inverted index and\ncalculate TF-IDF and BM25 for the same query.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a text collection to candidate documents<\/h2>\n<p>An inverted index maps each term to a postings list containing\ndocument IDs, potentially together with frequencies and positions.\nIntersecting two lists answers an AND query. Ranked retrieval also\nconsiders documents that match only some query terms. Positional\ninformation supports phrase search. Documents and queries must be\nanalysed consistently so that their terms are meaningfully\ncomparable.<\/p>\n<p>One definition of TF-IDF is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{td}=f_{td}\\log(N/df_t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{td}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts term occurrences,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">df_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts documents containing the term and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of documents. Other variants exist (logarithmic TF,\nsmoothed IDF and vector normalisation). BM25 reduces the effect of\nrepeated terms and adjusts for document length. For distinct query\nterms, we use this positive-IDF variant:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mover><mi>L<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(q,d)=\\sum_{t\\in q}\\log\\left(1+\\frac{N-df_t+0.5}{df_t+0.5}\\right)\\frac{(k_1+1)f_{td}}{f_{td}+k_1(1-b+b|d|/\\overline L)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_1&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontrols saturation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontrols length normalisation and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>L<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the mean document length. Other IDF and query variants are available.\nThe score is not a calibrated probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three workshop reports<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001624_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Grouped TF-IDF and BM25 bars rank document D1 ahead of D2 while D3 scores zero.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Grouped TF-IDF and BM25 bars rank\ndocument D1 ahead of D2 while D3 scores zero.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Lexical relevance scores.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>D1 = “pumpe pumpe dichtung”, D2 = “pumpe ventil”, D3 = “filter motor\nventil”. For “pumpe”,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">df=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>L<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline L=8/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe postings list contains D1:2 and D2:1. Unnormalised TF-IDF gives\napproximately 0.811 and 0.405. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_1=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nBM25 gives approximately 0.624 and 0.524. The second report benefits\nfrom being short, while the first benefits from its higher term\nfrequency. The diagram compares scores within each method.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>D<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[[<span class=\"st\">&#39;pumpe&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;pumpe&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;dichtung&#39;<\/span>],[<span class=\"st\">&#39;pumpe&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;ventil&#39;<\/span>],[<span class=\"st\">&#39;filter&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;motor&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;ventil&#39;<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[d.count(<span class=\"st\">&#39;pumpe&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> d <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> D]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> N<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(D)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> df<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> f)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>L<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>,D))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>idf<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>log(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(N<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>df<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(df<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[idf<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(k<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(d)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>L)) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x,d <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(f,D)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> s[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span>s[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span>s[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>([<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>log(N<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>df),<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> f],[<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> s])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ranking in practice<\/h2>\n<p>A search engine may weight fields differently, apply filters before\nor after retrieval, and rerank candidates. Exact matching is often more\nreliable than statistical token search for specific part numbers. Test\ncollections need genuine user queries or realistic tasks and independent\nrelevance judgements. Compare variants using the same collection;\nchanges to the collection also affect IDF values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the effect of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nLength dependence disappears. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe score equals IDF; with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.375<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(2.2)/(3.2)=1.375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes IDF. Even infinitely many term repetitions give at most a factor\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_1+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot unbounded growth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.elastic.co/docs/solutions/search/full-text/how-full-text-works\">Elastic:\nHow full-text search works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · documentation accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.emerald.com/ftinr/article-abstract/4/1-2/1/1326508/The-Probabilistic-Relevance-Framework-BM25-and?redirectedFrom=fulltext\">Robertson\nand Zaragoza (2009): The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and\nBeyond<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www-nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/\">Manning et\nal. (2008): Introduction to Information Retrieval<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · search snippet\nonly<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M24-L3 – Embeddings, Semantic Search and Ranking<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand dense vector\nretrieval, reranking and the evaluation of ordered search results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From words to semantic similarity<\/h2>\n<p>A dual encoder computes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=f(q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=g(d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseparately for a query and document. Document vectors can be stored\noffline. For non-zero vectors, cosine similarity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(u,v)=\\frac{u^\\top v}{\\|u\\|_2\\|v\\|_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For normalised vectors this equals the dot product; the squared\nEuclidean distance is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-2c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn encoder may be trained contrastively on suitable query–document pairs\nand negative examples. False negatives (documents that are actually\nrelevant) distort the training signal. Similarity is not the same as\nlogical entailment or factual agreement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three synthetic document vectors<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001614_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three document vectors have cosine similarities 0.8, 0.6 and 0 to the query.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three document vectors have cosine\nsimilarities 0.8, 0.6 and 0 to the query.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Cosine similarity in embedding space.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet A=(0.8, 0.6), B=(0.6, 0.8) and C=(0, 1) be three unit vectors. Their\nscores are 0.8, 0.6 and 0. The diagram shows the vectors and their\nprojection onto the query axis. Independently, suppose B and C are\nrelevant but A is not. Precision@2 and Recall@2 both equal 1/2, as does\nthe reciprocal rank of the first relevant document. For graded relevance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>@<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">DCG@k=\\sum_{i=1}^k(2^{r_i}-1)/\\log_2(i+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndividing by the ideally ranked DCG yields nDCG (provided IDCG is\npositive). With binary judgements, nDCG@2 is approximately 0.387\nhere.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> V<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>V<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(np.linalg.norm(V,axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.linalg.norm(u))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])[np.argsort(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s)][:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>discount<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.log2(np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ndcg<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>discount)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>discount.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(s,[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(ndcg<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.38685<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.0001<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(s,<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(ndcg,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multi-stage retrieval<\/h2>\n<p>For large collections, approximate nearest-neighbour indexes reduce\nquery latency at the potential cost of missing relevant results. Hybrid\nretrieval combines lexical and dense candidates, for example through\nrank fusion rather than by adding uncalibrated scores. A cross-encoder\njointly scores a query and document but is more expensive per pair. In a\ntwo-stage architecture, the initial index limits the recall achievable\nby subsequent reranking. Latency, memory, quality and filtering policies\nmust be tested together. Semantic similarity alone must not permit a\ndocument to bypass access controls.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the distance between u and A?\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.6325<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2-1.6}=\\sqrt{0.4}\\approx0.6325<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEven a perfect reranking of the existing top two can only move B into\nfirst place; it cannot recover C if C was never retrieved as a\ncandidate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10084\">Reimers and Gurevych\n(2019): Sentence-BERT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232\">Huang et al. (2025): A\nSurvey on Hallucination in Large Language Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\naccessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www-nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/\">Manning et\nal. (2008): Introduction to Information Retrieval<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · search snippet\nonly<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M24-L4 – Language Modelling and Autoregressive Training<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate autoregressive\nfactorisation, teacher forcing, cross-entropy and decoding outcomes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A sequence as a probability<\/h2>\n<p>By the chain rule, a language model assigns a probability to a token\nsequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1:T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x_{1:T})=\\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_\\theta(x_t\\mid x_{&lt;t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An initial token or context supplies the first conditioning input; an\nend-of-sequence token can also model sequence length. N-gram models\nlimit the history to a few tokens, whereas neural models use learned\nrepresentations of the preceding context up to their context limit. The\ntraining task for a causal model is next-token prediction. With teacher\nforcing, the preceding inputs come from the observed sequence rather\nthan the model’s own sampled outputs. The causal mask introduced in M19\nprevents access to future target positions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three prediction steps<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001709_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three token losses of 0.693, 1.386 and 0.223 nats yield a mean loss of 0.768.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three token losses of 0.693, 1.386 and\n0.223 nats yield a mean loss of 0.768.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Token loss contributions in language modelling.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For the synthetic token sequence “Ventil öffnen Ende” (“valve open\nend”), suppose the model assigns probabilities 0.5, 0.25 and 0.8 to the\ntokens actually observed. Their joint probability is 0.1, the negative\nlog-likelihood is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.3026<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log(0.1)\\approx2.3026<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the mean cross-entropy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7675<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=0.7675<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnats per token. Perplexity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.1544<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(H)\\approx2.1544<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an effective measure of uncertainty. The diagram shows the individual\nloss contributions. Comparing perplexities requires the same tokeniser,\ndata and masking procedure.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> loss<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>np.log(p)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(p.prod(),<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(np.exp(loss.mean()),<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(loss.mean(),<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(np.exp(loss.mean()),<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Generation is not training<\/h2>\n<p>At generation time, the current sequence is extended. Greedy decoding\nselects the most probable next token, whereas sampling draws from a\nprobability distribution. With logits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i\\propto\\exp(z_i/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nlower temperatures concentrate probabilities without adding factual\nknowledge. Top-k limits selection to k tokens, while top-p chooses the\nsmallest high-probability set with sufficient cumulative mass. The\nresulting distribution is then renormalised. Beam search explores\nseveral partial sequences but is still approximate, and its preference\nfor certain sequence lengths needs checking. Locally good decisions do\nnot necessarily yield the best complete sequence. A plausible sentence\nis not necessarily true.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Two tokens have probabilities 0.8 and 0.2. At half the temperature,\ntheir weights are squared and renormalised:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.64<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.64/(0.64+0.04)=16/17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe more probable token becomes likelier, but not certain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\">Brown et al. (2020):\nLanguage Models are Few-Shot Learners<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\">Vaswani et al. (2017):\nAttention Is All You Need<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~jurafsky/slp3/index.html\">Jurafsky and\nMartin: Speech and Language Processing, 3rd ed., online manuscript<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsearch snippet only<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M24-L5 – Encoders, Decoders and Transformer Language Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Select encoder, decoder and\nencoder–decoder models on the basis of their information flow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Different architectures, different objectives<\/h2>\n<p>The attention components introduced in M19 can be connected in\ndifferent ways. An encoder such as BERT typically processes input tokens\nwith bidirectional self-attention. In masked language modelling,\nselected token positions are altered and their original contents\npredicted. An encoder therefore suits classification, feature extraction\nand token labelling, but it is not automatically an autoregressive\ngenerator. A decoder-only model uses causal self-attention for\nnext-token prediction. An encoder–decoder model first processes the\ninput bidirectionally; the decoder additionally uses cross-attention\nover encoder outputs and causal self-attention over preceding target\ntokens. Architecture alone does not determine every training\nobjective.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Visibility in three matrices<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001512_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Encoder full attention, decoder causal triangular attention and rectangular cross-attention masks compared.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Encoder full attention, decoder causal\ntriangular attention and rectangular cross-attention masks\ncompared.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Attention visibility patterns.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The diagram uses three input tokens and two target positions. An\nunrestricted encoder mask permits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs. The causal decoder mask (including the diagonal, with shifted\ntarget inputs) permits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(3+1)/2=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs for three positions. Cross-attention from two target positions to\nthree input positions permits six pairs, provided no padding masks\napply. For an attention head with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\in\\mathbb R^{n_q\\times d_k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\in\\mathbb R^{n_k\\times d_k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\in\\mathbb R^{n_k\\times d_v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{softmax}(QK^\\top/\\sqrt{d_k}+M)V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nof shape\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_q\\times d_v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSoftmax operates row by row; disallowed pairs are masked before it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>E<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">bool<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> D<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.tril(E)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">bool<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (E.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(),D.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(),C.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>())<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>).reshape(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>k.T<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.exp(z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,keepdims<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">/=<\/span>a.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,keepdims<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(a<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>v,[[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>v)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Choosing and deploying a model<\/h2>\n<p>An encoder may be sufficient for searching reports for\noperating-state labels. A causal model suits free text continuation.\nEncoder–decoder architecture is a natural, though not exclusive, choice\nfor translation. With dense self-attention, the score matrix grows\nquadratically with sequence length. Caching keys and values can reduce\nrepeated computation during autoregressive generation, at the cost of\nadditional memory. Context length, data quality, training objective and\nevaluation task are distinct decision factors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many causally permitted pairs are there for four positions?\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2+3+4=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nout of 16. If two of five input positions are padding, each ordinary\ncross-attention row retains three valid key positions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805\">Devlin et al. (2019):\nBERT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\">Vaswani et al. (2017):\nAttention Is All You Need<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683\">Raffel et al. (2020):\nExploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified text-to-text\nTransformer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\">Brown et al. (2020):\nLanguage Models are Few-Shot Learners<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M24-L6 – Fine-Tuning, PEFT and Preference Optimisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish full fine-tuning,\nparameter-efficient fine-tuning and preference learning, and calculate\nLoRA parameter counts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adapting a model to a task<\/h2>\n<p>A pretrained model can be adapted to examples of desired responses\nthrough supervised fine-tuning (SFT). In many applications, the loss is\ncomputed only for response tokens, not for the input. Full fine-tuning\nupdates all weights selected for training. Parameter-efficient\nfine-tuning (PEFT) limits the trainable degrees of freedom, for example\nthrough inserted adapters, trainable prompt vectors or Low-Rank\nAdaptation (LoRA). In-context learning is different: examples appear in\nthe context without triggering a weight update.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A low-rank factorisation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001631_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Frozen base weight matrix and scaled product of two small trainable factors with 1,536 parameters.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Frozen base weight matrix and scaled\nproduct of two small trainable factors with 1,536\nparameters.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: LoRA low-rank parameter update.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mn>128<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_0\\in\\mathbb R^{128\\times256}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a frozen matrix, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times256}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mn>128<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\in\\mathbb R^{128\\times r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLoRA uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=W_0+(\\alpha/r)BA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe update needs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>128<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>256<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1536<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(128+256)=1536<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrainable matrix elements rather than 32768, or 4.6875% (excluding\nbiases and other layers). The diagram shows the shapes and sizes. The\nrank of BA is at most r; this does not guarantee that any particular\nlearning task can be solved adequately with that rank. Frozen weights\nstill incur computation and memory costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning from preferences<\/h2>\n<p>Preference learning seeks to favour preferred responses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover rejected responses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same input x. One RLHF approach first learns a reward model from\ncomparisons and then optimises a policy with regularisation towards a\nreference policy. Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO) instead uses a\npairwise loss with a frozen reference model:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=-\\log\\sigma\\left(\\beta\\left[\\log\\frac{\\pi_\\theta(y_w|x)}{\\pi_{ref}(y_w|x)}-\\log\\frac{\\pi_\\theta(y_l|x)}{\\pi_{ref}(y_l|x)}\\right]\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma(z)=1/(1+e^{-z})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor log ratios 0.4 and −0.2 with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe loss is about 0.4375. Preference datasets can contain social biases\nand unreliable judgements. Separate evaluation on domain questions,\nsafety cases and previously demonstrated abilities tests for undesirable\nside effects.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log1p,exp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> train<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">128<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">256<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> total<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">128<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">256<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>log1p(exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>))))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> train<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1536<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> train<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>total<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.046875<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(loss<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.437488<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(train,<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(loss,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How does the parameter count change at r=8? It becomes 3072, or\n9.375%. If the two DPO log ratios are equal,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=\\log2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregardless of their common value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685\">Hu et al. (2022):\nLoRA<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290\">Rafailov et al. (2023):\nDirect Preference Optimization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\">Brown et al. (2020):\nLanguage Models are Few-Shot Learners<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M24-L7 – Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Source Verification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model a RAG pipeline and\nevaluate retrieval quality, evidence faithfulness and source\nverification separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adding external knowledge<\/h2>\n<p>A workshop wants to query its manuals even though maintenance\nintervals can change. A language model has parametric knowledge;\nretrieval-augmented generation (RAG) additionally provides selected\nexternal passages during generation. Documents are ingested with\nprovenance, validity dates and access-control metadata, divided into\nsuitable chunks and indexed. Chunks that are too small lose context;\nthose that are too large dilute relevance. Overlap may help but\nincreases redundancy. When splitting tables, their headings and units\nmust be retained.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two revisions, one answer<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001674_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Flow from a query to retrieval, evidence validation and an answer; a clarification branch handles missing evidence.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Flow from a query to retrieval, evidence\nvalidation and an answer; a clarification branch handles missing\nevidence.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: RAG with source and version verification.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Our fictional manual A, revision 2, effective from 1 June 2026,\nspecifies for device P: “Check filter after 60 operating hours.”\nRevision 1 specifies 90 hours. Another passage describes cleaning the\ndevice. The query requests the current inspection interval. The\nretriever must consider access permissions and validity first, then\nreturn candidates; reranking prioritises a current, precisely relevant\npassage. The diagram shows the process, including abstention when\nevidence is absent. The answer “60 operating hours (A, Rev. 2)” is\nsupported here. “Every 60 days” would be wrong despite containing the\ncorrect number. An old manual alone does not justify a claim about\ncurrent requirements.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> datetime <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> date<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rows<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A1&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;2025-01-01&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">90<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A2&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;2026-06-01&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>asof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>date(<span class=\"dv\">2026<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>valid<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[r <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> date.fromisoformat(r[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>asof]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>chosen<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(valid,key<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> r:r[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],default<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> chosen<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A2&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;2026-06-01&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(chosen)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The example assumes that each new revision supersedes the previous\none for the same device and that no revision is withdrawn. In real\napplications, scope, withdrawal and version history must be modelled\nexplicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluation and trust<\/h2>\n<p>Evaluate retrieval recall for evidentiary passages, answer\ncorrectness, source precision (supported references among cited\nreferences), and source coverage (supported claims among claims\nrequiring citations) separately. A correctly retrieved passage can still\nbe interpreted incorrectly. Even a verbatim match may itself be wrong or\noutdated. Documents are data, not authorised instructions: embedded\nrequests for tool calls or disclosure must not override system rules.\nAccess checks must be enforced before content is passed to the model.\nWhen sources conflict or evidence is missing, the system should express\nuncertainty, ask a focused question or abstain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Three claims need supporting evidence, and two are supported by valid\npassages. Of four inserted references, three are relevant. Under the\ncounting rules defined here, source coverage is 2/3 and reference\nprecision is 3/4. These values say nothing by themselves about external\ntruth or the currency of every source.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\">Lewis et al. (2020):\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nabstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232\">Huang et al. (2025): A\nSurvey on Hallucination in Large Language Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\naccessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~jurafsky/slp3/index.html\">Jurafsky and\nMartin: Speech and Language Processing, 3rd ed., online manuscript<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsearch snippet only<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M24-L8 – Dialogue Systems, Tool Use and Evaluation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design dialogue state, tool\ncalls and end-to-end evaluation with controlled side effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a language model to a system<\/h2>\n<p>A dialogue system manages conversational context, the task,\npotentially long-term preferences and the available tools. Traditional\ntask-oriented systems separate intent recognition, slot filling,\ndialogue policy and response formulation. A language model may combine\nsome of these functions, but explicit state, permissions and boundaries\nare still required. A tool call is a structured proposal, not evidence\nthat an action has been successfully executed. Argument validation,\nauthorisation and interpretation of the actual return value belong to\nthe wider system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A controlled tool loop<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001735_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A model-proposed tool call is validated and either read access is permitted or the operation is rejected.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A model-proposed tool call is validated\nand either read access is permitted or the operation is\nrejected.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Access checks for model tool calls.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A fictional workshop query asks “How many P filters are stored in\nhall A?” The model proposes read_stock(Artikel=P, Halle=A). A backend\nvalidator checks recognised item codes and authorised halls, calls only\nthe read-only function and returns the stock count as data. Moving items\nbetween locations would require additional permissions and confirmed\nparameters. The diagram shows proposal, validation, tool, observation\nand response. A missing or ambiguous parameter triggers a clarification\nquestion. Timeouts, limits on the number of calls, repeatable read\noperations and idempotency keys for writes prevent uncontrolled loops\nand duplicate effects.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>stock<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{(<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>):<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,(<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>):<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> read_stock(args,allowed):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(args)<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span>{<span class=\"st\">&#39;article&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>} <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> args[<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>] <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> allowed:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;unzulässig&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    key<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(args[<span class=\"st\">&#39;article&#39;<\/span>],args[<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> key <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> stock:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;unbekannte Kombination&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> stock[key]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> read_stock({<span class=\"st\">&#39;article&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>},{<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>})<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    read_stock({<span class=\"st\">&#39;article&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>},{<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Zugriff verweigert&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Zugriffsschutz fehlt&#39;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>More than linguistic quality<\/h2>\n<p>Measure task completion, correctly validated calls, attempted\nunauthorised actions, cost, latency, clarification questions and error\nhandling. Evaluate at dialogue level rather than only per message if\nevery step is necessary to complete a task. Test missing arguments,\nmanipulated documents, contradictory results, network errors and\nrepeated proposals. Automated language-model judges can make mistakes;\nhuman sampling and reproducible tool states help make evaluations\nreliable. A simulated tool tests orchestration, not real backend\nsecurity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Of 20 dialogues, 15 end successfully. Three of the five remaining\ndialogues lack a required field, while two fail technically. The\ncompletion rate is 75%, the missing-slot rate 15% and the technical\nfailure rate 10% of this sample. An action that was merely proposed does\nnot count as completed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629\">Yao et al. (2023):\nReAct<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\">Lewis et al. (2020):\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nabstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232\">Huang et al. (2025): A\nSurvey on Hallucination in Large Language Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\naccessed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M24-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M24-L9 – Multilinguality, Hallucinations and System Limitations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Natural Language Processing and Information Retrieval<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Measure multilingual\nperformance, hallucinations and uncertainty separately, and make\nlimitations transparent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multilinguality is not a uniform capability<\/h2>\n<p>A jointly trained model can learn cross-lingual representations.\nDifferences in performance depend, among other factors, on the quantity\nof training data, domain, script, tokenisation and evaluation quality.\nMultilingual sentence vectors can be aligned in a shared space using\nparallel texts or distillation. Similarity scores for translation pairs\nindicate quality but do not guarantee a correct translation. Proper\nnames, numbers, decimal separators, units, negation and specialist\nterminology require targeted checks. An English-only benchmark is\ninsufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Per-language measurement and abstention<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001644_en.svg\"\nalt=\"German accuracy 90%, Spanish accuracy 50%, micro average 82% and macro average 70%.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">German accuracy 90%, Spanish accuracy\n50%, micro average 82% and macro average 70%.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Multilingual and aggregate accuracy.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a synthetic evaluation, there are 80 German questions and 20\nSpanish questions. The system answers 72 German and 10 Spanish questions\ncorrectly; initially, it answers every question. Micro-accuracy = 82/100\n= 82%, while macro-accuracy = (90% + 50%)/2 = 70%. The diagram shows the\ntwo language groups and both aggregated measures. Micro-accuracy\nconceals poorer performance in one language. Sample size, domain and\nuncertainty must also be reported. Selective answering may reduce risk\nwhen evidence is uncertain. In a separate second evaluation, a system\nanswers 60 of 100 questions, with 54 correct: coverage is 60%, accuracy\namong answered questions is 90%, and the error rate among answered\nquestions is 10%. Coverage and risk should be reported together.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ok<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">72<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> per<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>ok<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>micro<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>ok.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> macro<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>per.mean()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([micro,macro,<span class=\"dv\">54<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.82<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.70<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(per,micro,macro)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hallucinations and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A model may invent a claim that contradicts a reliable source, add an\nunsupported detail or fail to follow summarisation instructions. Factual\ncorrectness and faithfulness to the input are different dimensions. An\nunsupported claim is not necessarily demonstrably false. Token\nprobabilities measure the model’s preference for text continuations, not\nthe probability that a statement is true. Checking sources, calibrating\nuncertainty on an appropriate test set, using tools and involving human\nreview can limit risks, but cannot guarantee universal freedom from\nerror. Distribution shift and manipulated inputs can degrade\ncalibration. Also check context limits, truncated documents,\nprivacy-sensitive content and the distribution of errors across user\ngroups.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A third language is added with ten questions and eight correct\nanswers. Micro-accuracy = 90/110 ≈ 81.82%; macro-accuracy =\n(0.9+0.5+0.8)/3 ≈ 73.33%. The choice of aggregation must depend on the\napplication and be justified before evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09813\">Reimers and Gurevych\n(2020): Making Monolingual Sentence Embeddings Multilingual using\nKnowledge Distillation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232\">Huang et al. (2025): A\nSurvey on Hallucination in Large Language Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\naccessed<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\">Brown et al. (2020):\nLanguage Models are Few-Shot Learners<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract accessed<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
